WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.349
METR’s time horizon: turning benchmarks into a length.

2
00:00:03.629 --> 00:00:15.405
METR (Model Evaluation and Threat Research) proposed a measure in March 2025 (Kwa and colleagues) that has become the most cited single number in timeline arguments.

3
00:00:15.685 --> 00:00:22.811
The idea is to express capability as the length of task, in skilled human time, that an AI agent can complete.

4
00:00:23.091 --> 00:00:24.669
The method has three steps.

5
00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:37.984
First, assemble a suite of tasks (mostly software engineering, machine learning research, and cybersecurity) and time skilled humans doing them, so each task has a human duration from seconds to many hours.

6
00:00:38.264 --> 00:00:44.045
Second, run each AI agent on every task many times and record success or failure.

7
00:00:44.325 --> 00:00:51.749
Third, for each agent, fit a logistic curve of success probability against the logarithm of human task duration.

8
00:00:52.029 --> 00:01:00.669
The 50% time horizon is the task length where the fitted curve crosses one half; the 80% horizon is where it crosses 0.

9
00:01:00.949 --> 00:01:01.611
8.

10
00:01:01.891 --> 00:01:11.213
The 2025 paper reported that frontier 50% horizons had doubled roughly every seven months since 2019.

11
00:01:11.493 --> 00:01:14.821
The METR post on that paper is careful about what this means.

12
00:01:15.101 --> 00:01:27.667
It is a measured trend on one suite; extending it to “AI will do month-long projects by a given year” is an extrapolation that assumes the trend continues and generalizes beyond the suite.

13
00:01:27.947 --> 00:01:29.547
Note what the measure is not.

14
00:01:29.827 --> 00:01:36.717
It is not how long an agent runs; agents are usually several times faster than people on the tasks they complete.

15
00:01:36.997 --> 00:01:48.837
And it is not a measure of reliability at the level a business needs: the 80% horizon is much shorter than the 50% horizon, and nobody publishes a 99% horizon.
